    "result_text": "There's only one test class and the suite is green. \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the daily owner-creation limit: owner creation is rejected with **429 Too Many Requests** once 100 or more owners have already been registered today (by `registrationDate`).\n\nChanges, following the existing `isCityAtCapacity` capacity-check pattern:\n\n1. **`ClinicService.java`** \u2014 added `boolean isDailyRegistrationAtCapacity()` to the service facade, with a Javadoc describing the \"at most 100 owners created per day\" invariant.\n\n2. **`ClinicServiceImpl.java`** \u2014 added a `DAILY_CAPACITY = 100L` constant and implemented the method as a read-only transaction that counts owners whose `registrationDate` equals `LocalDate.now()` and returns whether that count has reached the limit (plus the `java.time.LocalDate` import).\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 added a guard that returns `HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS` when the daily limit is reached, before the owner is saved.\n\nThe acceptance test `coreRejectsOverDailyLimit` (creates 100 owners dated today, then expects the 101st to be rejected) passes, and the full suite reports `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\nNote: new owners get `registrationDate` stamped to the current date via the entity's `@PrePersist`, so the count naturally reflects owners created today. The limit applies to the `POST /api/owners` (v1) create path, which is the only owner-creation endpoint.",
